      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack discipline, but because the document is asked to do something impossible: promise the future in an environment defined by uncertainty. The failure is structural, and it recurs in predictable ways.\n\n**They confuse output with outcome.** The typical roadmap is a list of features arranged against dates. Shipping that list is treated as success, even when the features change nothing about customer behavior or business performance. A roadmap that says \"Q3: notifications redesign\" makes no claim that can be proven wrong. One that says \"reduce first-week churn by 15%\" does. The first invites busywork; the second invites judgment about whether the work is worth continuing.\n\n**They are political settlements, not strategies.** In many organizations, the roadmap is the negotiated peace between sales, marketing, support, and executive pet projects. Each stakeholder receives a slot in exchange for cooperation. The result is a document with no spine: everything is a priority, nothing is a bet, and there is no stated rationale that would justify saying no to the next urgent request. A roadmap without a strategy behind it is just a wishlist with quarters attached.\n\n**They mistake estimates for commitments.** A date twelve months out is a guess with enormous error bars, but once it appears in a slide deck it hardens into a promise. Sales sells it. Executives plan around it. When reality intervenes, teams face a choice between missing the commitment and shipping something hollow to satisfy it. They usually choose the latter, which quietly trains everyone that roadmap items get delivered in name only.\n\n**They ignore the true cost of running a product.** Plans are drawn as if capacity were fully available for new work, when in practice a substantial share goes to maintenance, incidents, security, compliance, onboarding, and technical debt. The roadmap is therefore overcommitted from the moment it is approved, and the overflow is absorbed by delay or by cutting quality.\n\n**They resist learning.** The purpose of building is partly to discover whether your assumptions were right. Yet once an item is on the roadmap, momentum and sunk cost keep it there. Evidence that a bet is failing arrives, and the plan does not move. Meanwhile, plans that are never revisited drift from reality until people stop consulting them altogether — the roadmap becomes theater, maintained for review meetings and ignored in daily decisions.\n\n**They serve too many audiences at once.** Engineers need sequencing and dependencies. Executives need to see how work ladders up to company goals. Customers need reassurance without specific dates. Forcing one artifact to satisfy all three guarantees it satisfies none.\n\nThe remedy is not to abandon planning but to change what the roadmap asserts. Organize it around problems and measurable outcomes rather than features. Express confidence honestly — near-term work specified, later work deliberately vague. Publish the reasoning, so the plan can be argued with. Revisit it on a fixed cadence, and treat removing items as evidence of learning rather than failure. A roadmap should be a statement of intent that survives contact with new information — not a contract that punishes anyone for discovering the truth.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nProduct roadmaps often fail because they are asked to do something impossible: make an uncertain future look certain. A tidy sequence of features and release dates creates confidence, but it can hide the assumptions, trade-offs, and unanswered questions that determine whether a product succeeds.\n\nThe first problem is confusing delivery with progress. A roadmap might promise a redesigned dashboard, a mobile app, and three integrations. Shipping all five does not necessarily make the product more useful or the business more sustainable. When teams are judged by whether they deliver planned features, they optimize for completion rather than impact. The roadmap becomes a checklist instead of a strategy.\n\nThe second problem is premature commitment. Early estimates are made when teams know the least about technical complexity, customer needs, and dependencies. Yet tentative dates can quickly become sales promises, budget assumptions, and executive expectations. As evidence accumulates, changing direction becomes politically expensive. Teams continue building something questionable because removing it would look like failure—even when stopping would be the better decision.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they substitute negotiation for prioritization. Sales wants features that close deals. Support wants recurring complaints resolved. Leadership wants visible growth initiatives. Engineering wants to reduce technical debt. Each request may be reasonable, but combining them does not automatically produce a coherent plan. Without a clear target customer, desired outcome, and basis for saying no, the roadmap becomes a record of who argued most effectively.\n\nAnother weakness is treating the roadmap as a document rather than an ongoing decision process. Customer behavior changes, competitors move, and experiments disprove assumptions. A plan that cannot absorb those discoveries becomes obsolete. But constant, unexplained changes are equally damaging: they leave teams distracted and stakeholders distrustful. Adaptability requires both regular review and a clear explanation of what changed and why.\n\nBetter roadmaps do not eliminate uncertainty; they make it manageable. They connect proposed work to measurable outcomes, distinguish near-term commitments from longer-term possibilities, and identify assumptions that need testing. They also acknowledge capacity constraints and leave room for maintenance, discovery, and unexpected problems.\n\nMost importantly, a useful roadmap communicates choices rather than merely announcing activity. It explains which problems matter now, why they matter, and what evidence would justify a different direction.\n\nA roadmap fails when following it becomes more important than achieving its purpose. It succeeds when it helps an organization coordinate its efforts while preserving the ability to learn. The goal is not to predict every turn in the road, but to keep moving toward a destination worth reaching.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
